# Line endings are decided here, not by whichever machine has the checkout.
#
# This file did not exist until 2026-08-31, and the cost of that showed up the
# first time the tree moved between machines: the working copy was carried over
# from a Windows box rather than cloned fresh, so every one of 241 tracked text
# files arrived with CRLF against an index holding LF. `git status` reported
# 41184 insertions and exactly 41184 deletions, and not one byte of it was a
# real change. Nothing was broken — but every diff, every review and every
# `git add -A` was unreadable until it was undone.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text (it sniffs for NUL bytes), and
# `eol=lf` pins what lands in the working tree on every platform. The explicit
# `-text` lines below are for formats where that sniff is not worth trusting:
# a false positive there does not produce a noisy diff, it corrupts the file.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries. Never normalise, never diff as text.
*.png     -text
*.jpg     -text
*.jpeg    -text
*.gif     -text
*.webp    -text
*.ico     -text
*.icns    -text
*.svg     text eol=lf
*.ttf     -text
*.otf     -text
*.woff    -text
*.woff2   -text
*.mp4     -text
*.mp3     -text
*.wav     -text
*.m4a     -text
*.pdf     -text
*.zip     -text
*.jar     -text
*.so      -text
*.dylib   -text
*.jks     -text
*.keystore -text
*.p12     -text
*.apk     -text
*.aab     -text
*.ipa     -text

# The reference captures the golden tests compare against. Recorded on one
# machine and compared byte-for-byte; a newline rewrite would fail every one of
# them with no visible cause.
.codex/design-qa/** -text

# The sticker artwork, in LFS.
#
# `design-previews` is 314 MB of tracked drawings — seventy-two animations at
# 512 px across six versions, plus the sheets they were built from. None of it
# is in the app: `tool/build_sticker_assets.py` re-encodes what ships into
# `assets/stickers`, and this is the source it reads. It is here so the
# artwork is not only on one laptop.
#
# **`assets/stickers` is deliberately NOT here.** Those files are bundled into
# the APK, and the workflows check out without LFS — which does not fail, it
# writes a 130-byte pointer where each file should be. The build would then
# ship pointers as stickers and say nothing. If that ever needs to change, the
# checkout steps need `lfs: true` first, and in the same commit.
#
# Note on cost: a free GitHub account gets 1 GB of LFS storage and 1 GB of
# bandwidth a month. This is about a third of it, and re-pushing these files
# spends more, so treat a new version of the artwork as something that costs
# rather than something that is free.
design-previews/**/*.webp filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
design-previews/**/*.gif  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
design-previews/**/*.png  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
design-previews/**/*.mp4  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
design-previews/**/*.webm filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Windows-only sources. Checked in for the desktop target that exists for UI
# work, and edited on Windows when they are edited at all — CRLF is what
# Visual Studio writes and there is no reason to fight it.
*.sln     text eol=crlf
*.vcxproj text eol=crlf
*.props   text eol=crlf
*.bat     text eol=crlf
*.ps1     text eol=crlf
